.TH CLOG_FLUSH 3 "2026-08-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_flush \- block until queued log records are durably written
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

void clog_flush(clog logger);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Blocks the calling thread until write(2) has durably written two
things.  The first is every record that something queued through logger
at the moment of this call.  A sibling handle that shares the same
target below counts as well.  The second is whatever an aggregation
buffer holds that is only part filled.

This call does nothing and returns at once for a synchronous logger,
which is one with no async mode.  Such a logger is one that somebody
created with no clog_async_cfg_t.  See clog_open_fd_mp(3) and
clog_open_file_mp(3).

Another thread can submit a record at the same time as this call, and
strictly after it.  This call does not promise to cover that record.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
logger  Logger handle.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
It is safe to call this on any handle that is still open, at any time.
That covers a call at the same time as an ordinary log_*() call, on the
same handle or on a sibling handle.
The async queue below can fail to accept the flush request, on a rare
allocation failure.  This function then returns at once and does not
block.  It does not wait for an acknowledgment that will never arrive.
In a forked child, a logger whose async mode has fallen back to
synchronous logging does nothing here.  See clog_open_fd_mp(3).  The
call behaves exactly as it does on a synchronous logger.
A write can stop early with an error that a later attempt can
outlast, such as ENOSPC.  The bytes that it did not take stay in the
aggregation buffer, and the next flush offers them again.  This call
can therefore return while part of what it flushed is still pending.
After any other write error, such as EPIPE, the flush drops those
bytes and writes a record that names the loss.  See clog_open_fd_mp(3)
for both rules and for what they promise about the framing of a
record.
Asserts when logger is CLOG_INVALID, stale or already closed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_open_fd_mp (3),
.BR clog_open_file_mp (3),
.BR clog_close (3)
